#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382530 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c6e6984-7029-5e3e-bfcc-b8e553658911" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Non-Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5830ab83-4536-518d-9ee2-3906d7f12d2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+u(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382532} {:answer/id #uuid "fbf7d96f-5625-5a72-8c43-cc611a413676" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-3u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382533} {:answer/id #uuid "86822d51-08a3-5414-b2fb-65f2ef3af1be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=2{u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382534} {:answer/id #uuid "e3376030-8837-55f9-948f-0ff849889be8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382535} {:answer/id #uuid "0b771851-2d66-5637-aed2-ba61f79f9680" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3376030-8837-55f9-948f-0ff849889be8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382535} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6d81a5e-5920-5bb5-a87d-a682ea9af8c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382531}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "67ef18c6-5d45-520b-b960-5f958b495e82" :question/math-academy-id "q-335377" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+3x(t)+y(t)+1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+2y(t)-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[-1,\\,1]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)-1,\\,y(t)=v(t)+1,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+3x(t)+y(t)+1 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)-1)^{2}+3(u(t)-1)+(v(t)+1)+1 \\\\  & =({u}^{2}(t)-2u(t)+1)+3u(t)-3+v(t)+1+1 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =-3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+2y(t)-4 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =-3(u(t)-1)-(v(t)+1)^{2}+2(v(t)+1)-4 \\\\  & =-3u(t)+3-({v}^{2}(t)+2v(t)+1)+2v(t)+2-4 \\\\  & =-3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c6e6984-7029-5e3e-bfcc-b8e553658911" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Non-Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30697ebe-2afa-5c29-8ac7-a4cbbf3dcad2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2{u}^{2}(t)+v(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382539} {:answer/id #uuid "2930cd30-1bb8-596f-a39b-2c8e9e036c92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=2{u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+{v}^{2}(t)+3v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382540} {:answer/id #uuid "dd876892-fd02-54e5-8eaf-3f51b45b948b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+{v}^{2}(t)+3v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+v(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382541} {:answer/id #uuid "43da4ee8-a24d-53c6-a82a-c66d6f3f0b4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+v(t)-3{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382542} {:answer/id #uuid "c2c89baf-dc51-55da-a0ed-800398f60484" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd876892-fd02-54e5-8eaf-3f51b45b948b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+{v}^{2}(t)+3v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+v(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382541} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac906d6e-9252-5102-a285-d574e59dd762" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6291bca-1fab-53ea-a875-cc77eae942da" :question/math-academy-id "q-335380" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+{y}^{2}(t)-3x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+5y(t)-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[1,\\,2]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)+1,\\,y(t)=v(t)+2,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+{y}^{2}(t)-3x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)+1)^{2}+(v(t)+2)^{2}-3(u(t)+1)-(v(t)+2) \\\\  & =({u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+1)+({v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)+4)-3u(t)-3-v(t)-2 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+{v}^{2}(t)+3v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =2x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+5y(t)-8 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =2(u(t)+1)-(v(t)+2)^{2}+5(v(t)+2)-8 \\\\  & =2u(t)+2-({v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)+4)+5v(t)+10-8 \\\\  & =2u(t)+v(t)-{v}^{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+{v}^{2}(t)+3v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+v(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382544 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bd72f8f-010f-58c8-97b5-420df1cbb6e3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Shifted System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f758aa4-8093-5259-b9f8-c2fe57294638" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{w}_{2}-{x}^{∗}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382546} {:answer/id #uuid "cd72f633-253a-5a6c-b1d1-b80eaaf8a938" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{w}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382547} {:answer/id #uuid "5c3e92c5-0226-51a9-a45f-762a4ec9fd95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382548} {:answer/id #uuid "dc51427f-7c45-5b35-8884-d8ed02848ea2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{w}_{1}+{x}^{∗}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382549} {:answer/id #uuid "ce2a422e-6000-5c61-ae9c-bc9f48b43c65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{∗}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd72f633-253a-5a6c-b1d1-b80eaaf8a938" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{w}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382547} :answer-field/id #uuid "617d1b37-248a-582a-ab0c-2e6a110238e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8027b2a7-781d-5bbf-89b4-b685be3303c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-335484" :question/problem "A linear system of ODEs has the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}=[2,\\,3]^{T}.$ Two linearly independent eigenvectors of the system's matrix are ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}.$ After shifting using $u=x-{x}^{∗},$ the system becomes ${u}^{′}=Bu.$ Which of the following is **guaranteed** to be an eigenvector of the matrix $B?$" :question/worked-solution "After shifting a linear system, the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors remain unchanged.\n\nThus, the matrix $B$ in the shifted system has the same eigenvectors as the system's matrix in the original coordinates. These eigenvectors are ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2},$ or any nonzero vectors that are parallel to them.\n\nAmong the given options, the only vector that is nonzero and is guaranteed to be parallel to either ${w}_{1}$ or ${w}_{2}$ is the vector $5{w}_{1}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bd72f8f-010f-58c8-97b5-420df1cbb6e3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Shifted System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34e7b2e2-7a71-56c3-a40c-910ff5b33f80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382553} {:answer/id #uuid "e81ea147-132c-5167-b69b-6c1b94ccad60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382554} {:answer/id #uuid "fb2f8738-ef9c-5fbb-9ca3-3e4c609d4f99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382555} {:answer/id #uuid "68395084-33b8-52ab-b604-df5add6ccc41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382556} {:answer/id #uuid "319bfb5c-df15-5bf6-b7da-ea28dbada0e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68395084-33b8-52ab-b604-df5add6ccc41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382556} :answer-field/id #uuid "213f80d0-d4db-5f3c-9afe-0cc9861f9dc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6d7f5c3c-3dd8-541e-aac4-0b9cacaa8d19" :question/math-academy-id "q-335488" :question/problem "A linear system of ODEs has an equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}.$ One of the nullclines of the system has the equation $y=-4x+1$ in the corresponding phase space. The system is then shifted using $u=x-{x}^{∗}.$ Which of the following is **guaranteed** to be a nullcline of the shifted system?" :question/worked-solution "After shifting a system, the geometric structure of the vector field is preserved. In particular, each nullcline is translated but retains its orientation, meaning its slope does not change.\n\nBecause the equilibrium is moved to the origin by the shift, the translated nullclines must pass through the origin in the shifted coordinates.\n\nThus, the nullclines of the shifted system pass through the origin and have the same slopes as the corresponding nullclines of the original system.\n\nAmong the given options, the only line that satisfies the above conditions is $y=-4x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bd72f8f-010f-58c8-97b5-420df1cbb6e3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Shifted System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d66401ff-7ed9-5260-9758-2c79da328ca9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An asymptotically stable spiral sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382560} {:answer/id #uuid "142319a6-5eeb-5942-8298-84b4ddfbc7e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An unstable saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382561} {:answer/id #uuid "2ea4054b-47dc-564a-977c-fdb7368428fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An asymptotically stable sink (stable node)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382562} {:answer/id #uuid "c19d6940-8bb4-5e7b-8d7a-7676b055ac81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An unstable spiral source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382563} {:answer/id #uuid "137fffe5-a4b0-58ae-8b45-03f178846249" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stable center" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "142319a6-5eeb-5942-8298-84b4ddfbc7e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An unstable saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382561} :answer-field/id #uuid "2aa542cd-2462-5f97-a5e9-feb37f93ef1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a43edc38-467a-59cb-be35-534236235f78" :question/math-academy-id "q-335515" :question/problem "A system of ODEs has an unstable saddle point at ${x}^{∗}.$ The system is then shifted using $u=x-{x}^{∗}.$ Which of the following best describes the point $(0,0)$ for the shifted system?" :question/worked-solution "Shifting does not alter the local behavior near an equilibrium. So, the equilibrium retains its original classification (saddle, node, spiral, center, etc.).\n\nRecall that the shift moves the equilibrium to the origin.\n\nThus, the equilibrium at $(0,0)$ in the shifted system has the same classification as ${x}^{∗}$ in the original system.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"An unstable saddle point\"."}]}
